\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Total inversion of the massive Wilson kernel}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{For \vocabk{graded total inversion}{fdip-019C}, take a
\vocabk{connection}{fdip-006D} on the
\vocabk{finite incidence presentation}{fdip-0002} with
vertices #{\Lambda} and positive edges #{(x,j)} from #{x}
to #{x+e_j}. Write #{U_j(x)} for the represented unitary
transport. For #{N_j\ge3} these are the positive
nearest-neighbor edges of the
\vocabk{covariant lattice Dirac operator}{fdip-009O}. For #{N_j=2},
#{(x,j)} and #{(x+e_j,j)} have independent links: neither
is the formal reverse of the other. For #{N_j=1} the edge
is a loop, whose formal reversal carries the inverse transport.
Total inversion reverses every labeled direction, even when
reduction modulo a period fixes its endpoint sites.}

\p{Put
##{U^b_j(x)=U_j(-x-e_j)^{-1}.}
At real spacing #{a>0} let #{D_{a,U}} be the centered operator,
#{L_{a,U}} the Laplacian with #{-L_{a,U}\ge0}, and
#{K_U=m\bbI+D_{a,U}-c_W L_{a,U}} with real #{m,c_W},
as in the \vocabk{Wilson operator}{fdip-009U}. Then
##{\begin{aligned}
 U_- D_{a,U} U_-^{-1}&=D_{a,U^b},&
 U_- L_{a,U} U_-^{-1}&=L_{a,U^b},\\
 U_- K_U U_-^{-1}&=K_{U^b},&
 \det K_U&=\det K_{U^b}.
\end{aligned}}
On a connection class closed under #{U\mapsto U^b}, any
scalar gauge weight satisfying #{w_g(U^b)=w_g(U)} therefore
gives the same product #{w_g(U)\det K_U} at both connections.}

\proof{
\p{Suppress identity factors on tensor products. The forward
shift is #{(T_j^U\psi)(x)=U_j(x)^{-1}\psi(x+e_j)} and its
inverse has coefficient #{U_j(x-e_j)} on #{\psi(x-e_j)}.
Conjugating the forward shift by #{b^*} gives the backward
value at #{x-e_j} with coefficient #{U_j(-x)^{-1}}.
This is exactly #{U^b_j(x-e_j)}, so
#{b^*T_j^U(b^*)^{-1}=(T_j^{U^b})^{-1}}.
The prescribed reversal sends the positive step based at
#{x} to the formal reverse of the positive step based at
#{-x-e_j}. It retains that choice when the signed site
images coincide. Thus the positive link uses the reflected
head as the base of its inverse transport at every positive
period, including parallel steps and loops. The spin grading
commutes with
both shifts and negates every direction.}

\p{Each centered summand is #{\varepsilon_j
(T_j^U-(T_j^U)^{-1})/(2a)}. Its two sign changes cancel.
The Laplace summand #{(T_j^U+(T_j^U)^{-1}-2\bbI)/a^2}
is symmetric under exchanging the shifts. Their unitarity
gives #{2\bbI - T-T^{-1}=(\bbI - T)^\dagger(\bbI - T)} for
each shift #{T}, giving the stated sign of #{L_{a,U}}.
Taking the linear combination gives similarity for #{K_U}.
Multiplicativity of determinant cancels the change of field
variables, including when the kernel is singular. Multiplying
by the specified invariant factor gives the weight identity.
Closure under the final inversion suffices. A fixed
connection requires its own links #{U^b=U}.}
}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{LinearMap.det_conj} gives invariance of
the determinant of an endomorphism conjugated by a linear
equivalence. The reflected-link calculation supplies that
equivalence here.}
